---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import Layers from '../../components/Layers.astro';
import { scenes } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Essential Dignities', href: '/explore/dignities/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Essential Dignities — domicile, exaltation, detriment, and fall"
  description="The traditional table of planetary strength: which planets are at home, exalted, in detriment, or in fall in each sign — and what it actually means for reading a chart."
  path="/explore/dignities/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Essential dignities</h1>
        <p class="lede">Traditional astrology asks a question modern astrology often skips: is this planet in a position to do its job well? The dignity tables are how it answers.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.chartDetail.src}
        alt={scenes.chartDetail.alt}
        width={scenes.chartDetail.width}
        height={scenes.chartDetail.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <p>
        A planet's <strong>essential dignity</strong> describes how well-placed it is by sign —
        whether it can express its nature freely or has to work against the grain. It is one of the
        oldest ideas in Western astrology and one of the most useful, and it largely fell out of
        popular practice during the twentieth century. Mo works with it, which is why you'll see it
        on every planet and sign page here.
      </p>

      <h2>The four essential dignities</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Domicile (rulership)</strong> — the planet is in its own sign. It is at home: it
          has full access to its own resources and behaves most characteristically.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Exaltation</strong> — the planet is an honoured guest. Strong and well-regarded,
          though in a slightly formal, borrowed way. Each exaltation has a specific degree of
          maximum strength.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Detriment</strong> — the planet sits opposite its own sign. It is working away
          from home, in conditions that don't suit its method.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fall</strong> — opposite its exaltation. The planet is at its least supported;
          traditional texts describe it as being without honour.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The table</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Essential dignities table, scrollable">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <caption>Essential dignities of the seven traditional planets (Ptolemaic degrees)</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Planet</th>
            <th scope="col">Domicile</th>
            <th scope="col">Exaltation</th>
            <th scope="col">Detriment</th>
            <th scope="col">Fall</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="glyph-cell" aria-hidden="true">☉</span> Sun</th><td>Leo</td><td>Aries 19°</td><td>Aquarius</td><td>Libra</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="glyph-cell" aria-hidden="true">☽</span> Moon</th><td>Cancer</td><td>Taurus 3°</td><td>Capricorn</td><td>Scorpio</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="glyph-cell" aria-hidden="true">☿</span> Mercury</th><td>Gemini, Virgo</td><td>Virgo 15°</td><td>Sagittarius, Pisces</td><td>Pisces</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="glyph-cell" aria-hidden="true">♀</span> Venus</th><td>Taurus, Libra</td><td>Pisces 27°</td><td>Scorpio, Aries</td><td>Virgo</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="glyph-cell" aria-hidden="true">♂</span> Mars</th><td>Aries, Scorpio</td><td>Capricorn 28°</td><td>Libra, Taurus</td><td>Cancer</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="glyph-cell" aria-hidden="true">♃</span> Jupiter</th><td>Sagittarius, Pisces</td><td>Cancer 15°</td><td>Gemini, Virgo</td><td>Capricorn</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="glyph-cell" aria-hidden="true">♄</span> Saturn</th><td>Capricorn, Aquarius</td><td>Libra 21°</td><td>Cancer, Leo</td><td>Aries</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
      <p class="card__meta">
        The modern planets — Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto — have no traditional dignities, since the
        system predates their discovery. Some modern astrologers assign them by analogy; traditional
        practitioners do not.
      </p>

      <Layers kind="misconception">
        <p>
          That a planet in detriment or fall is "bad." It describes difficulty of expression, not
          moral quality or doom — and some of the most interesting placements in a chart are planets
          that had to work for their competence. A debilitated planet that a person has consciously
          developed frequently outperforms a dignified one they've never thought about.
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <h2>Why it changes a reading</h2>
      <p>
        Dignity gives you a way to weigh a chart rather than simply describe it. Two people can both
        have Mars in the tenth house; if one has Mars in Capricorn (exalted) and the other has Mars
        in Cancer (fall), those are meaningfully different capacities for sustained professional
        drive. Without dignity you can only say "Mars in the tenth" twice.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also gives structure to <em>rulership chains</em>. Every house is ruled by the planet
        that governs the sign on its cusp, and that planet's own condition — including its dignity —
        colours everything the house describes. This is the backbone of traditional chart judgment
        and the reason
        <a href="/readings/natal/">a classical reading</a> feels different from a purely
        psychological one.
      </p>

      <Layers kind="differs">
        <p>
          Modern psychological astrology largely set dignity aside, on the grounds that it implies
          judgment about people rather than description. Traditional and Hellenistic revival
          practitioners consider it indispensable. Both positions are held by serious astrologers,
          and it's a fair question to ask whoever is reading your chart.
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <h2>Beyond the essentials</h2>
      <p>
        The full traditional system also includes <em>accidental dignity</em> — strength from
        circumstance rather than sign: whether a planet is angular, direct or retrograde, fast or
        slow, visible or hidden by the Sun's rays, and which house it occupies. And there are finer
        divisions within each sign (terms, faces, and triplicity rulers) that add further gradation.
        Those belong to a deeper study than a single page, but the four dignities above carry most
        of the practical weight.
      </p>
      <p>
        Related: <a href="/explore/planets/">the planets</a>,
        <a href="/explore/signs/">the signs</a>, and
        <a href="/explore/schools/">schools and traditions</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand heading="Want your chart read with a classical lens?" body="Dignity, rulership, and sect are part of how Mo reads — not decoration." buttonLabel="About Mo's approach" buttonHref="/approach/" />
</BaseLayout>
